//! The admission gate (PORT_PLAN.md D6): all-or-nothing, at combat start.
//!
//! The engine's totality argument has two halves. This module is the first:
//! it walks the entry's whole reachable content closure — every card identity
//! in every pile, every monster kind's whole move loop, every power on every
//! creature, the continuation stack, the phase marker — and refuses the fight
//! unless *all* of it is inside the implemented registry. The second half is
//! that every function in [`super`] is total on what survives this walk, so a
//! mid-fight "not modeled" can only come from a state the engine itself built,
//! which is a bug rather than a gap.
//!
//! Two properties this gate is built for:
//!
//! * **It lists everything, not the first thing.** [`AdmissionRefusal`] carries
//!   the complete missing set, so an operator reading one refusal learns the
//!   whole distance to admission rather than peeling it off one PR at a time.
//! * **The registry is derived, never transcribed.** The implemented kind sets
//!   below are the single source of truth for both this walk and the dispatch
//!   arms in [`super::play`] / [`super::turn`]; a `match` there and a set here
//!   that disagreed would show up as a mid-fight refusal, which
//!   [`super::tests`] pins against.
//!
//! Argument *shapes*: the gate holds a central opinion only for the R0.5
//! slice-1 kinds (whose bodies unpack through the same [`step_args`] /
//! [`move_args`] the gate calls — one compiled reading, I4). Every other
//! implemented kind's shape is **body-owned** (#1351/#1353): the body
//! destructures `ctx.args` and returns a typed refusal on surprise, which the
//! differential's `must_cover` makes pre-merge-visible. See
//! [`central_step_shape_refusal`].
//!
//! # Compile at admission (#1297)
//!
//! The generated tables carry step and move arguments as heterogeneous tuples,
//! and some of those arguments are **strings**: pile names, `select` filter
//! modes, sub-mode selectors. D2 says no dispatch below the canonical boundary
//! may compare text, and the tables satisfy that rule only if something turns
//! those strings into typed values before play begins. That something is here:
//! [`step_word_classes`] / [`move_word_classes`] publish the position-addressed
//! rules, [`crate::catalog::compile_args`] applies them while the catalog
//! interns each row, and the play path is handed
//! [`crate::catalog::CompiledStep`]s whose arguments are ids, enums and
//! integers only. A string that no rule and no generated vocabulary resolves
//! becomes [`crate::catalog::CompiledArg::Unresolved`], which [`admit`] reports
//! as [`MissingCapability::UnknownWord`] — named, never guessed at.
//!
//! # The capability manifest (D6)
//!
//! [`capability_manifest`] is **derived**: the implemented step and move kinds
//! come from the per-family `IMPLEMENTED` registries the generated dispatch
//! trees concatenate, so a family that fills a body and forgets to list it
//! stays refused, and a family that lists a kind it did not implement is caught
//! by the differential. Nothing about the manifest is transcribed, which is the
//! whole point of replacing the kernel's hardcoded per-encounter manifests.

